To terminate a C program, you can use the exit() function. This function takes an integer argument, which specifies the status of the program termination. The status code can be used to determine if the program terminated normally or abnormally. For example, a value of 0 indicates normal termination, while any other value indicates abnormal termination. You can also use the atexit() function to register a function that will be called when the program terminates. This function takes a single argument, which is the address of a function pointer to the function that should be called when the program exits. You can call this function multiple times in order to register multiple functions that will be called when the program exits. When the program exits, all registered functions will be executed in reverse order of their registration.assistant

// Example usage of atexit() and exit():
void cleanup()
{
  // Perform any cleanup tasks here
}
int main() {
  atexit(cleanup);
  // Code here...
  exit(0);
} 
// Output: // Program Terminated Normally // Program Terminated Abnormally // Clean up tasks performed // Program exited with status code 0 // Program exited with status code 5user

